      "subtitle": "The lab's",
      "body": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "statements-close",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "Proximity operations,\nclose approaches,\nand shadowing",
      "subtitle": "Official statements",
      "body": "In different contexts, States describe these activities as concerning."
    },
    {
      "id": "statements-shadow",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "Six voices on proximity",
      "subtitle": "",
      "body": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "statements-focus",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "Physical parameters as a basis for concern",
      "subtitle": "A closer look",
      "body": "Observed dynamics can help establish what is unusual. Whether they are disturbing also depends on context that States may not share. What physical basis of concern could we describe in common?"
    },
    {
      "id": "tle",
      "kind": "chapter",
      "title": "How to describe an orbit",
      "subtitle": "01 · Orbital elements",
      "body": "A satellite’s orbit can be described by six elements. Adjust them to see how its path changes around Earth."
    },
    {
      "id": "uncertainty",
      "kind": "chapter",
      "title": "Orbital uncertainty",
      "subtitle": "02 · What a TLE does not say",
      "body": "The ellipsoid is a hypothetical uncertainty region around the estimated satellite position. Public TLEs generally provide no covariance, so its size and shape are not measured uncertainty."
    },
    {
      "id": "neighbors",
      "kind": "chapter",
      "title": "Two neighboring GEO satellites",
      "subtitle": "03 · Shared orbital region",
      "body": "Object A and Object B occupy nearby geostationary longitudes. Their TLE estimates can be updated as observations arrive."
    },
    {
      "id": "relative",
      "kind": "chapter",
      "title": "Relative motion",
      "subtitle": "04 · One satellite's frame",
      "body": "Object B relocates while Object A anchors the rotating view. Separation distance, relative speed, and range rate answer different questions."
    },
    {
      "id": "conjunction",
      "kind": "chapter",
      "title": "Conjunction versus close approach",
      "subtitle": "06 · Different questions",
      "body": "Compare a brief, faster encounter with sustained slower proximity. Collision screening and descriptions of behavior have different purposes."
    },
    {
      "id": "library",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "Episodes of Concerning Satellite Proximity",
      "subtitle": "Historical library",
      "body": "Explore documented episodes, orbital reconstructions, and linked source records."
    },
    {
      "id": "project-page",
      "kind": "project",
      "title": "How Close Is Too Close?",
      "subtitle": "Project page",
      "body": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "limitations",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "Limits of public reconstructions",
      "subtitle": "What the model cannot establish",
      "body": "Sparse observations, dated TLEs, unknown covariance, and unobserved maneuvers limit what a public reconstruction can claim."
    },
    {
      "id": "future",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "Future research",
      "subtitle": "From episodes to baselines",
      "body": "Congestion, statistical baselines, and proximity between energy-related space systems."
    },
    {
      "id": "future-examples",
      "kind": "chapter",
      "title": "Further behavioral questions",
      "subtitle": "Future research",
      "body": "Energy-space proximity and sustained line of sight are distinct from separation distance alone."
    },
    {
      "id": "discussion",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "Discussion",
      "subtitle": "Questions for the room",
      "body": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "discussion-questions",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "Questions for discussion",
      "subtitle": "OEWG on PAROS",
      "body": "Which measurable characteristics of satellite behavior are most useful for distinguishing routine proximity operations from activities that may warrant concern, clarification, or consultation?\n\nWhere might states’ interpretations of close approaches, shadowing, and other proximity operations converge or diverge, and what additional operational context is necessary to understand those differences?\n\nWhat information could states reasonably share to substantiate their concerns or enable independent assessments, without disclosing sensitive space domain awareness capabilities?\n\nHow might shared physical descriptions and evidentiary practices contribute to voluntary transparency and confidence-building measures, legally binding instruments, or both?"
    },
    {
      "id": "closing",
      "kind": "slide",
      "title": "How Close Is Too Close?",
      "subtitle": "Side event",
      "tagline": "Building Shared Understanding of Satellite Behavior",
      "body": "OEWG on PAROS, Fourth Session · November 23, 2026"
    }
